{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ונקלה", :translit "v'niklah", :gloss "and shall be degraded"} {:he "ונקלה כבוד מואב", :translit "v'niklah kevod mo'av", :gloss "and the glory of Mo'av shall be degraded"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ונקלה" "ונקלה כבוד"]}], :frame {:text "The word ונקלה appears in exactly two contexts in Scripture: here in Deuteronomy 25:3 regarding the degraded punishment of the wicked, and in Isaiah 16:14 — 'ונקלה כבוד מואב' — regarding the diminution of Mo'av's glory (Isaiah 16:14). The Ba'al HaTurim reads a linguistic link between the two: since the word ונקלה connotes being lessened or degraded, one must ensure that even a wicked person receiving lashes is not degraded beyond what the Torah prescribes.", :sources ["Isaiah 16:14" "Deuteronomy 25:3"]}} {:quotes [{:he "אחיך", :translit "achicha", :gloss "your brother"} {:he "זבל", :translit "zavel", :gloss "spoiled/ruined"} {:he "ברעי", :translit "b're'i", :gloss "by its beating"} {:he "פטור", :translit "patur", :gloss "exempt"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "אחיך", :b "זבל"}], :frame {:text "The word אחיך in the phrase 'your brother' (Deuteronomy 25:3) is numerically equivalent to זבל, meaning 'spoiled' or 'ruined.' This alludes to the law that if the wicked person being punished deteriorates or is spoiled by the beating — that is, receives more lashes than his body can endure — the one administering the punishment is exempt. The Torah's limit of forty lashes is set precisely so that 'your brother' — who remains your brother even after sinning — is not destroyed.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 25:3"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ונקלה אחיך לעיניך", :translit "v'niklah achichah l'einecha", :gloss "and your brother shall be degraded before your eyes"} {:he "לא תחסום", :translit "lo tachsom", :gloss "you shall not muzzle"} {:he "לפני השמש", :translit "lifnei ha-shamesh", :gloss "before the sun-baker"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Adjacent parashah sections (סמוכים) hint that the court officer administering lashes must receive food provisions."}], :frame {:text "The Ba'al HaTurim reads the juxtaposition of 'ונקלה אחיך לעיניך' — which he parses as 'before your eyes,' i.e., before the שמש, the court attendant who oversees the punishment — with the immediately following passage 'לא תחסום שור בדישו' (Deuteronomy 25:4) as a hermeneutical link. Just as the ox threshing grain may not be muzzled but must eat from the produce it works with, so too the שמש who carries out the court's labor must be provided sustenance from every type of food available.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 25:3" "Deuteronomy 25:4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ונקלה" "ונקלה כבוד"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ונקלה", :refs ["Deuteronomy 25:3" "1 Samuel 18:23" "Isaiah 16:14"], :n 3, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ונקלה כבוד", :refs ["Isaiah 16:14"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :gematria, :a "אחיך", :a-value 39, :b "זבל", :b-value 39, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :note "Adjacent parashah sections (סמוכים) hint that the court officer administering lashes must receive food provisions.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:39:16.310835720Z", :model "glm-5.2"}